>Does anyone besides me feel almost happy when their pet dies?

No! In the early game your pet is your best friend and protector.

As most folks know I generally run the Minetown Protection Run (MPR) in
the early game. The pet is essiential equipment during the run. Pet tasks
include:

1) Killing nasties
2) Being a HP buffer
3) Stealing from shops
4) Not cursed item detection (especially armor)
5) Trap finder

A XL9 72 HP maxed out pet is a formidable foe to everything in the early
dungeon except for maybe watch captains.

Pets can then morph in the midgame by using poly traps. Getting cool pets
like Lichs are an added benefit.

The biggest two problems are the fact that they stop to eat, and that they
aren't real good about staying close. Poly traps can help with the first
and magic whistles are invaluable for the second. But during the MPR both 
are somewhat good because it forces thinking and patience.

>Personally I feel it takes a lot of burden off me, what with not having
>to take care of him and make sure he comes downstairs with you and
>whatnot.

You never have to be bothered with a pet unless you want to be. Simply leave
them on a level without you for awhile. The pet will eventually become untame.
Also if the pet is domesticated you can always retame by feeding or make
peaceful by throwing a food item they don't eat.

So you can choose how you want to manage pets.

As for me, they are my best friends. When I lose a pet, I generally spend time
finding and buffing up another one as soon as I come across one.

